WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
Aisha sees an online post promising that a life policy is

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.400
a risk-free savings account with guaranteed large returns.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.860
A polished design does not make that description accurate.

4
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.360
Maryland prohibits untrue, deceptive or misleading insurance advertising and

5
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.840
misleading policy descriptions. Identify the product as life insurance,

6
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.320
explain material conditions and separate guarantees from assumptions.

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.580
The same honesty belongs in a conversation, a social post and

8
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:31.640
a presentation. An attractive result based on a possible future crediting

9
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.340
rate should never be presented as a contractual promise.

10
00:00:34.914 --> 00:00:40.674
An illustration can project guaranteed and nonguaranteed policy values

11
00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:43.414
over time. The labels matter.

12
00:00:44.694 --> 00:00:48.214
Maryland requires the applicable guaranteed elements to appear before

13
00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:54.274
corresponding nonguaranteed elements. Nonguaranteed

14
00:00:54.274 --> 00:00:59.134
assumptions can change, and actual results can be higher or lower.

15
00:00:59.974 --> 00:01:04.474
Suppose a projected dividend is expected to help pay a future

16
00:01:04.474 --> 00:01:08.954
premium. That projection does not make the policy paid up.

17
00:01:09.794 --> 00:01:14.814
The rules prohibit misleading vanishing-premium language when it implies

18
00:01:14.814 --> 00:01:19.274
that nonguaranteed values eliminate the continuing obligation.

19
00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:23.774
Show what happens if the assumed results do not occur.

20
00:01:24.828 --> 00:01:28.548
If a policy is applied for as illustrated, the signed basic

21
00:01:28.548 --> 00:01:32.508
illustration goes to the insurer with the application and a copy

22
00:01:32.508 --> 00:01:33.668
goes to the applicant.

23
00:01:34.428 --> 00:01:39.188
If the insurer issues different terms, a revised illustration must match

24
00:01:39.188 --> 00:01:44.428
the issued policy. It is labeled revised and signed and dated

25
00:01:44.428 --> 00:01:49.528
no later than delivery, with copies for the insurer and policyowner.

26
00:01:49.988 --> 00:01:52.468
Do not keep showing a more attractive old version.

27
00:01:52.468 --> 00:01:57.628
If no conforming illustration was used at application, follow the

28
00:01:57.628 --> 00:02:01.028
certification and delivery procedure required for that situation.

29
00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:05.742
The buyer's guide gives general help with understanding and comparing life

30
00:02:05.742 --> 00:02:10.622
insurance. A policy summary supplies the specified costs and guaranteed

31
00:02:10.622 --> 00:02:12.862
benefits of the particular policy.

32
00:02:13.322 --> 00:02:17.542
An illustration has its own rules for depicting values over time.

33
00:02:18.242 --> 00:02:22.262
Maryland generally requires the buyer's guide before acceptance of the

34
00:02:22.262 --> 00:02:27.142
initial premium. If the policy has an unconditional refund provision of

35
00:02:27.142 --> 00:02:30.842
at least ten days, the guide may instead arrive with or

36
00:02:30.842 --> 00:02:35.782
before policy delivery. For covered forms not marketed with an illustration,

37
00:02:36.602 --> 00:02:39.962
the required policy summary follows the same delivery timing.

38
00:02:40.728 --> 00:02:45.488
Elena collects income and other private information for a life application.

39
00:02:46.628 --> 00:02:50.188
That does not make the information available for any marketing purpose.

40
00:02:51.028 --> 00:02:55.948
Maryland's financial-privacy rules generally require a clear initial notice

41
00:02:55.948 --> 00:02:58.348
when the customer relationship starts.

42
00:02:59.148 --> 00:03:04.248
Before certain disclosures to a nonaffiliated third party, the licensee must

43
00:03:04.248 --> 00:03:09.650
provide the required notices and a reasonable opportunity to opt out.

44
00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.928
The customer must not have opted out.

45
00:03:12.708 --> 00:03:17.388
Exceptions exist for specified transaction servicing and other lawful

46
00:03:17.388 --> 00:03:22.508
purposes. Identify the information, recipient and

47
00:03:22.508 --> 00:03:25.488
purpose before deciding which rule applies.

48
00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:31.234
Annual privacy notices are generally part of a continuing customer

49
00:03:31.234 --> 00:03:36.314
relationship, but Maryland has an exception when

50
00:03:36.314 --> 00:03:40.694
disclosures are limited to the specified permitted categories and the

51
00:03:40.694 --> 00:03:44.274
disclosed policies and practices have not changed.

52
00:03:44.974 --> 00:03:50.294
Nonpublic health information has a separate authorization rule with listed

53
00:03:50.294 --> 00:03:55.314
insurance-function exceptions, including underwriting and claims

54
00:03:55.314 --> 00:03:59.274
work. That matters even in a life-insurance application.

55
00:04:00.414 --> 00:04:03.694
An exception for a real underwriting task is not permission to

56
00:04:03.694 --> 00:04:06.614
post a client's diagnosis in a sales chat.

57
00:04:07.634 --> 00:04:12.314
Use approved systems, limit access and preserve the required records.
